  "strategy": "# 1. Approach and rationale\nThe proposed approach combines firefly optimization with adaptive kernel smoothing to explore the solution space more effectively than previous methods. Unlike the parent solution's fixed block structures, our method uses a swarm-based search strategy that automatically adapts to solution quality. Each \"firefly\" represents a candidate step function, with brightness proportional to its overlap score. We introduce switchable kernels to balance exploration/exploitation and employ a fuzzy edge smoother to maintain feasibility. This approach addresses the parent's limitations in handling non-convexity and escaping local optima while maintaining mathematical rigor.\n\n# 2. Mathematical specification\nWe represent h as a piecewise function with variable breakpoints and heights:\n\n$$h(x) = \\sum_{i=1}^m a_i \\phi(x; c_i, w_i)$$\n\nwhere:\n- $a_i \\in [0, 1]$ are coefficients controlling the height\n- $\\phi(x; c_i, w_i)$ is a basis function with center $c_i$ and width $w_i$\n- $m$ is the number of free parameters, determined adaptively\n\nThe objective function is:\n\n$$C_5 = \\max_k \\int_{0}^{2} h(x)(1 - h(x + k)) dx$$\n\nsubject to:\n1. $\\int_{0}^{2} h(x) dx = 1$\n2. $0 \\leq h(x) \\leq 1$ for all x\n\nWe use a combination of discrete cosine and Gaussian kernels to form the basis functions, allowing smooth transitions between block structures.\n\n# 3. Implementation procedure\n## Preprocessing and setup\n1. Set n_points = 256, dx = 2.0 / n_points\n2. Initialize firefly population:\n   - num_fireflies = 50\n   - Each firefly represents a candidate h with random breakpoints and heights\n   - Initial positions scaled to satisfy sum(h) = n_points / 2\n3. Define kernel types (cosine and Gaussian) and their parameters\n4. Set firefly algorithm parameters:\n   - alpha = 0.5 (randomization)\n   - beta = 1.0 (attractiveness)\n   - gamma = 0.1 (light absorption)\n   - max_iter = 1000\n\n## Firefly optimization loop\nFor each iteration:\n1. Evaluate each firefly's objective:\n   - Compute overlap integral using np.correlate\n   - Record brightness proportional to C5 score\n2. Sort fireflies by brightness\n3. Perform firefly attraction:\n   - For each firefly, move towards the brighter ones\n   - Update positions using:\n     $$x_{new} = x_{old} + \\beta e^{-\\gamma r} (x_{bright} - x_{old}) + \\alpha \\epsilon$$\n   - where r is inter-firefly distance, \u03b5 ~ N(0,1)\n4. Apply kernel smoothing:\n   - Alternate between cosine and Gaussian kernels based on current iteration\n   - Smoothing level: \u03b7 = 0.1 * (1 - iter/max_iter)\n5. Project to feasible region:\n   - Clip values to [0,1]\n   - Rescale to maintain sum(h) = n_points / 2\n6. Handle failures by resetting deviant fireflies\n\n## Post-processing\n1. Select top 5 candidates\n2. Refine each with SLSQP:\n   - Initial guess: firefly position\n   - Constraints: sum(h)=1 and 0 \u2264 h \u22641\n   - Run for max_iter = 200\n3. Choose the best among refined candidates\n\n## Termination\n1. Stop after max_iter or time budget exhausted\n2. Return best candidate\n\n# 4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Kernel selection: Start with Gaussian (good for exploration), switch to cosine (good for exploitation)\n- Smoothness control: \u03b7 varies from 0.1 to 0\n- Population size: 50 provides good balance between diversity and runtime\n- Firefly parameters:\n   - alpha = 0.5 (randomness)\n   - beta = 1.0 (attractiveness)\n   - gamma = 0.1 (light falloff)\n\n# 5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Firefly optimization loop: ~800s\n- SLSQP refinement: ~200s\n- Total: ~1000s\n\n## Robustness measures\n- Automatic kernel switching based on phase\n- Feasibility projection after each step\n- Worst-case fallback to parent solution\n- Built-in early termination for failed optimizations\n\n# 6. Validation and return contract\n1. Check sum(h) \u2208 [0.5-1e-6, 0.5+1e-6]\n2. Verify all h[i] \u2208 [0,1]\n3. Recompute C5 using evaluator to confirm consistency\n4. Ensure no NaNs or infinities in candidate h\n5. Return h, C5, and n_points",
